1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:03,300
Drop a pinch of dust into a moving

2
00:00:03,340 --> 00:00:06,020
stream, and keep your eyes on two places.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:08,890
Here, the dust spreads apart.

4
00:00:09,260 --> 00:00:11,360
Grains that started as neighbors drift

5
00:00:11,480 --> 00:00:13,360
away from each other, as if water were

6
00:00:13,440 --> 00:00:14,990
welling up from somewhere underneath.

7
00:00:15,990 --> 00:00:18,320
And here, the dust starts to turn.

8
00:00:18,620 --> 00:00:20,500
The little cloud swings around, as

9
00:00:20,580 --> 00:00:22,220
if it caught the edge of a whirlpool.

10
00:00:23,340 --> 00:00:24,860
Those two motions have names.

11
00:00:25,290 --> 00:00:26,750
The first is divergence.

12
00:00:27,130 --> 00:00:28,400
The second is curl.

13
00:00:29,420 --> 00:00:30,540
Most of us meet them as a

14
00:00:30,660 --> 00:00:32,010
row of partial derivatives.

15
00:00:32,220 --> 00:00:34,220
Partial P, partial x,

16
00:00:34,340 --> 00:00:36,080
plus partial Q, partial y.

17
00:00:36,630 --> 00:00:37,900
You learn the pattern, you get

18
00:00:37,940 --> 00:00:39,580
the right answers on the exam, and

19
00:00:39,660 --> 00:00:41,340
the picture never quite shows up.

20
00:00:42,340 --> 00:00:43,720
So let's put the picture first.

21
00:00:44,180 --> 00:00:45,840
Each of these is really a question you

22
00:00:45,920 --> 00:00:48,220
can ask about any single point in a flow.

23
00:00:48,660 --> 00:00:49,850
Is stuff leaving this point?

24
00:00:50,230 --> 00:00:51,810
And is stuff turning around it?

25
00:00:52,840 --> 00:00:54,700
Answer the first question everywhere,

26
00:00:54,740 --> 00:00:55,940
and you get a map of where the

27
00:00:56,020 --> 00:00:57,920
flow is born and where it disappears.

28
00:00:58,360 --> 00:01:00,340
Answer the second, and you find every

29
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:02,260
hidden spin, even in places where

30
00:01:02,340 --> 00:01:03,810
the arrows look perfectly straight.

31
00:01:04,780 --> 00:01:06,280
Then something nice happens when

32
00:01:06,360 --> 00:01:08,130
you add up all those tiny answers.

33
00:01:08,520 --> 00:01:09,960
Two of the biggest theorems in

34
00:01:10,080 --> 00:01:11,980
calculus fall out, almost for free.

35
00:01:12,950 --> 00:01:14,060
We will start with arrows on

36
00:01:14,140 --> 00:01:15,900
a page, and end with the four

37
00:01:16,020 --> 00:01:18,210
equations behind every radio, every

38
00:01:18,300 --> 00:01:20,000
motor, and every beam of light.

39
00:01:25,060 --> 00:01:26,267
Pick a point in the plane,

40
00:01:26,293 --> 00:01:27,410
and attach an arrow to it.

41
00:01:27,900 --> 00:01:29,150
The arrow could mean anything.

42
00:01:29,570 --> 00:01:31,120
The speed of the wind, the pull of

43
00:01:31,480 --> 00:01:33,430
gravity, the push of an electric charge.

44
00:01:34,360 --> 00:01:35,940
Do that at every point, and

45
00:01:36,020 --> 00:01:37,300
you have a vector field.

46
00:01:37,640 --> 00:01:38,820
A rule that hands each

47
00:01:39,100 --> 00:01:40,250
location its own arrow.

48
00:01:41,210 --> 00:01:43,450
A weather map of the wind is exactly this.

49
00:01:43,850 --> 00:01:45,110
Every town gets an arrow,

50
00:01:45,330 --> 00:01:46,440
telling you which way the air

51
00:01:46,520 --> 00:01:48,070
is moving there, and how fast.

52
00:01:49,020 --> 00:01:50,380
To write it down, split

53
00:01:50,460 --> 00:01:51,920
each arrow into two parts.

54
00:01:52,310 --> 00:01:54,540
How far it points to the right, we call P.

55
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:57,060
How far it points up, we call Q.

56
00:01:57,520 --> 00:01:59,050
Both depend on where you are.

57
00:01:59,420 --> 00:02:01,580
So the field is F of x y

58
00:02:01,780 --> 00:02:03,790
equals P of x y, Q of x y.

59
00:02:04,820 --> 00:02:06,330
Arrows alone are hard to read.

60
00:02:06,630 --> 00:02:07,960
So let them carry something.

61
00:02:08,380 --> 00:02:10,480
Sprinkle dust everywhere, and let each

62
00:02:10,560 --> 00:02:12,290
grain move along the arrow underneath it.

63
00:02:13,240 --> 00:02:14,720
Now the field is a flow.

64
00:02:15,120 --> 00:02:16,660
The arrows give the velocity of the

65
00:02:16,820 --> 00:02:18,660
water at each point, and the dust

66
00:02:18,740 --> 00:02:20,160
shows you where the water goes.

67
00:02:21,110 --> 00:02:22,610
Three flows will keep us company.

68
00:02:23,050 --> 00:02:24,520
In the first, every arrow

69
00:02:24,680 --> 00:02:25,870
points away from the center.

70
00:02:26,240 --> 00:02:27,290
Call it the source.

71
00:02:27,690 --> 00:02:29,240
In the second, every arrow

72
00:02:29,400 --> 00:02:30,410
points around the center.

73
00:02:30,740 --> 00:02:31,710
Call it the whirl.

74
00:02:32,100 --> 00:02:33,640
And in the third, water comes

75
00:02:33,720 --> 00:02:35,400
in from above and below, and

76
00:02:35,480 --> 00:02:36,730
leaves to the left and right.

77
00:02:37,150 --> 00:02:38,340
That one is a saddle.

78
00:02:39,300 --> 00:02:40,340
Keep an eye on the saddle.

79
00:02:40,600 --> 00:02:41,930
It is going to fool us in a minute.

80
00:02:43,680 --> 00:02:44,900
Here is the first question.

81
00:02:45,190 --> 00:02:47,480
Drop a small circle of dye into the

82
00:02:47,760 --> 00:02:49,800
flow, and ask what happens to its area.

83
00:02:50,880 --> 00:02:52,460
In the source, every grain

84
00:02:52,540 --> 00:02:53,990
runs away from every other.

85
00:02:54,440 --> 00:02:55,460
The circle grows.

86
00:02:55,840 --> 00:02:58,330
Before long, its area has nearly tripled.

87
00:02:59,460 --> 00:03:01,200
In the whirl, the circle swings

88
00:03:01,280 --> 00:03:02,480
around the center, but it

89
00:03:02,560 --> 00:03:03,960
never gets bigger or smaller.

90
00:03:04,360 --> 00:03:06,320
Its area stays exactly the same.

91
00:03:07,440 --> 00:03:08,270
Now the saddle.

92
00:03:08,760 --> 00:03:10,500
The dye gets pulled out sideways

93
00:03:10,580 --> 00:03:12,380
and squeezed from above and below.

94
00:03:12,820 --> 00:03:14,540
Before you watch, make a guess.

95
00:03:14,900 --> 00:03:16,680
Does the area grow, or shrink?

96
00:03:19,740 --> 00:03:20,600
It does neither.

97
00:03:21,030 --> 00:03:22,280
The circle stretches into

98
00:03:22,440 --> 00:03:23,800
a long, thin ellipse.

99
00:03:24,180 --> 00:03:25,110
It looks dramatic.

100
00:03:25,480 --> 00:03:27,800
But it gets longer exactly as fast as

101
00:03:27,840 --> 00:03:30,110
it gets thinner, and the area stays put.

102
00:03:31,040 --> 00:03:32,760
That is what divergence measures.

103
00:03:33,070 --> 00:03:34,630
Not how wild the flow looks.

104
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:36,860
Only how fast a tiny drop is

105
00:03:36,940 --> 00:03:38,520
swelling, compared to its size.

106
00:03:38,950 --> 00:03:40,670
Positive means the drop grows.

107
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:42,430
Negative means it shrinks.

108
00:03:42,800 --> 00:03:45,160
Zero means the flow only rearranges it.

109
00:03:46,190 --> 00:03:48,160
To turn that into a formula, shrink

110
00:03:48,200 --> 00:03:49,920
the drop to a tiny square, and

111
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:51,600
count the water crossing its edges.

112
00:03:51,990 --> 00:03:53,080
Whatever flows out,

113
00:03:53,120 --> 00:03:54,380
minus whatever flows in.

114
00:03:55,300 --> 00:03:57,010
Start with the left and right edges.

115
00:03:57,390 --> 00:03:58,740
Only the sideways part of the

116
00:03:58,820 --> 00:04:01,140
arrows, P, carries water across them.

117
00:04:01,560 --> 00:04:03,700
If P is a little bigger on the right edge

118
00:04:03,780 --> 00:04:06,250
than on the left, more leaves than enters.

119
00:04:06,630 --> 00:04:08,260
That difference, divided by the

120
00:04:08,340 --> 00:04:09,940
width of the square, is the partial

121
00:04:10,020 --> 00:04:12,120
derivative of P with respect to x.

122
00:04:13,020 --> 00:04:14,600
The top and bottom edges work the

123
00:04:14,720 --> 00:04:17,373
same way, with the upward part, Q.

124
00:04:17,373 --> 00:04:18,440
More leaving through the top

125
00:04:18,520 --> 00:04:19,720
than entering through the bottom

126
00:04:19,760 --> 00:04:21,570
gives partial Q, partial y.

127
00:04:22,470 --> 00:04:24,650
Add the two, and that is the divergence.

128
00:04:24,950 --> 00:04:27,260
Del dot F equals partial P partial

129
00:04:27,580 --> 00:04:29,440
x, plus partial Q partial y.

130
00:04:30,400 --> 00:04:31,730
Check it on our three flows.

131
00:04:32,090 --> 00:04:33,760
The source gives one plus one.

132
00:04:34,130 --> 00:04:34,430
Two.

133
00:04:34,820 --> 00:04:36,600
The whirl gives zero plus zero.

134
00:04:36,890 --> 00:04:39,040
And the saddle gives one, minus one.

135
00:04:39,330 --> 00:04:39,800
Zero.

136
00:04:40,200 --> 00:04:41,760
The formula sees exactly

137
00:04:41,800 --> 00:04:42,770
what the dye showed us.

138
00:04:43,680 --> 00:04:45,220
Now do it at every point,

139
00:04:45,300 --> 00:04:46,120
and color the plane.

140
00:04:46,430 --> 00:04:47,780
Warm where the divergence is

141
00:04:48,140 --> 00:04:49,740
positive, cool where it is negative.

142
00:04:50,150 --> 00:04:51,820
The warm spots are sources,

143
00:04:51,860 --> 00:04:52,810
where the flow comes from.

144
00:04:53,150 --> 00:04:54,780
The cool spots are sinks,

145
00:04:54,820 --> 00:04:55,630
where it drains away.

146
00:04:56,020 --> 00:04:57,170
And the dust agrees.

147
00:04:57,450 --> 00:04:59,420
It spreads out over the warm patches,

148
00:04:59,460 --> 00:05:01,330
and crowds together over the cool ones.

149
00:05:02,250 --> 00:05:04,360
One more flow, and this one matters later.

150
00:05:04,740 --> 00:05:06,440
The arrows all point away from the

151
00:05:06,520 --> 00:05:08,600
center again, but they shrink as you move

152
00:05:08,680 --> 00:05:10,720
outward, like light fading from a lamp.

153
00:05:11,550 --> 00:05:12,940
Everything points outward.

154
00:05:13,210 --> 00:05:15,030
So the divergence must be positive.

155
00:05:15,300 --> 00:05:15,530
Right?

156
00:05:18,410 --> 00:05:19,720
The map stays dark.

157
00:05:19,930 --> 00:05:21,520
Everywhere, except the

158
00:05:21,600 --> 00:05:22,970
single point at the center.

159
00:05:23,350 --> 00:05:24,890
Look at a circle around it.

160
00:05:25,320 --> 00:05:27,307
Make the circle twice as wide,

161
00:05:27,333 --> 00:05:29,653
and its edge is twice as long.

162
00:05:29,653 --> 00:05:31,660
But the arrows out there are half as long.

163
00:05:32,070 --> 00:05:34,400
The two effects cancel, so the same

164
00:05:34,480 --> 00:05:36,440
amount of water crosses every circle.

165
00:05:36,710 --> 00:05:38,460
Nothing is created out here.

166
00:05:38,960 --> 00:05:40,960
All of it comes from that one point.

167
00:05:41,890 --> 00:05:42,700
Hold on to that.

168
00:05:43,100 --> 00:05:43,770
It comes back.

169
00:05:45,900 --> 00:05:47,130
Now the second question.

170
00:05:47,660 --> 00:05:50,110
This time, drop in a tiny paddle wheel,

171
00:05:50,370 --> 00:05:52,280
pinned in place so it can only spin.

172
00:05:53,090 --> 00:05:54,830
In the whirl, it turns.

173
00:05:55,150 --> 00:05:57,460
Steadily, counterclockwise, at the

174
00:05:57,580 --> 00:05:59,180
same rate no matter where you put it.

175
00:06:00,340 --> 00:06:01,260
Now a river.

176
00:06:01,830 --> 00:06:03,630
Every arrow points straight to the right.

177
00:06:03,990 --> 00:06:05,880
But the water near the top moves

178
00:06:05,920 --> 00:06:07,480
faster than the water near the bottom.

179
00:06:08,010 --> 00:06:08,860
Will the wheel spin?

180
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:13,127
It does.

181
00:06:13,127 --> 00:06:14,860
The top paddles get pushed harder than the

182
00:06:14,940 --> 00:06:17,370
bottom ones, so the wheel turns clockwise.

183
00:06:17,690 --> 00:06:19,670
Straight arrows, and still a spin.

184
00:06:20,700 --> 00:06:21,980
The reverse can happen too.

185
00:06:22,440 --> 00:06:23,390
Here is a drain.

186
00:06:23,650 --> 00:06:25,040
The arrows go around in

187
00:06:25,160 --> 00:06:26,700
circles, faster near the middle.

188
00:06:26,990 --> 00:06:28,800
Pin a wheel anywhere away from

189
00:06:28,880 --> 00:06:31,200
the center, and it does not turn.

190
00:06:31,200 --> 00:06:32,480
Let it float with the water, and

191
00:06:32,520 --> 00:06:33,840
it travels all the way around

192
00:06:33,920 --> 00:06:35,410
the drain, but stays upright,

193
00:06:35,680 --> 00:06:36,990
like a car on a Ferris wheel.

194
00:06:37,350 --> 00:06:39,280
The fast water on the inside

195
00:06:39,320 --> 00:06:40,400
and the slow water on the

196
00:06:40,480 --> 00:06:42,320
outside balance out perfectly.

197
00:06:43,350 --> 00:06:44,980
So curl is not about whether

198
00:06:45,060 --> 00:06:46,260
the arrows go in circles.

199
00:06:46,470 --> 00:06:48,527
It is about whether a tiny wheel,

200
00:06:48,553 --> 00:06:50,140
sitting right here, would turn.

201
00:06:50,550 --> 00:06:51,380
And how fast.

202
00:06:52,250 --> 00:06:54,680
To measure it, walk once around a tiny

203
00:06:54,760 --> 00:06:57,240
square, counterclockwise, and add up how

204
00:06:57,360 --> 00:06:59,030
much the flow pushes you along the way.

205
00:06:59,550 --> 00:07:01,580
That total is called the circulation.

206
00:07:02,610 --> 00:07:04,300
Along the bottom edge you walk to the

207
00:07:04,380 --> 00:07:06,990
right, so the sideways part, P, helps you.

208
00:07:07,330 --> 00:07:08,940
Along the top you walk to the

209
00:07:09,020 --> 00:07:11,740
left, so P works against you.

210
00:07:11,740 --> 00:07:13,420
If P is bigger on top, you

211
00:07:13,500 --> 00:07:14,730
lose more than you gain.

212
00:07:15,010 --> 00:07:16,220
That difference becomes

213
00:07:16,420 --> 00:07:18,030
minus partial P, partial y.

214
00:07:19,090 --> 00:07:20,930
On the right edge you walk up, and

215
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:22,933
on the left edge you walk down.

216
00:07:22,987 --> 00:07:24,720
So the upward part, Q, gives

217
00:07:24,800 --> 00:07:26,440
you partial Q, partial x.

218
00:07:27,390 --> 00:07:28,090
Put them together.

219
00:07:28,330 --> 00:07:30,340
The curl is partial Q partial

220
00:07:30,580 --> 00:07:32,710
x, minus partial P partial y.

221
00:07:33,120 --> 00:07:35,140
Circulation, per unit of area.

222
00:07:36,150 --> 00:07:36,490
Check it.

223
00:07:36,840 --> 00:07:39,410
The whirl gives one, minus negative one.

224
00:07:39,910 --> 00:07:40,210
Two.

225
00:07:40,550 --> 00:07:42,680
The river gives zero, minus one.

226
00:07:42,910 --> 00:07:43,550
Negative one.

227
00:07:43,950 --> 00:07:45,510
Clockwise, just like the wheel.

228
00:07:45,840 --> 00:07:47,880
The source and the saddle give zero.

229
00:07:48,120 --> 00:07:49,000
No spin at all.

230
00:07:49,970 --> 00:07:51,990
In three dimensions, a wheel can tilt.

231
00:07:52,230 --> 00:07:53,820
So the curl becomes a vector.

232
00:07:54,080 --> 00:07:55,980
It points along the axle that makes

233
00:07:56,060 --> 00:07:57,740
the wheel spin fastest, and the

234
00:07:57,820 --> 00:07:59,980
right hand rule picks the direction.

235
00:07:59,980 --> 00:08:01,660
Curl your fingers with the spin, and

236
00:08:01,780 --> 00:08:03,230
your thumb points along the curl.

237
00:08:04,120 --> 00:08:05,520
Our flat curl was only

238
00:08:05,600 --> 00:08:06,800
one piece of that vector.

239
00:08:07,050 --> 00:08:08,320
The piece pointing straight

240
00:08:08,400 --> 00:08:09,520
up, out of the page.

241
00:08:10,600 --> 00:08:11,380
There is a famous

242
00:08:11,460 --> 00:08:12,620
shorthand for all of this.

243
00:08:12,850 --> 00:08:15,480
Write del as a list of three derivatives.

244
00:08:15,860 --> 00:08:18,430
Partial x, partial y, partial z.

245
00:08:18,730 --> 00:08:20,580
Take its dot product with F,

246
00:08:20,870 --> 00:08:21,990
and you get the divergence.

247
00:08:22,400 --> 00:08:23,727
Take its cross product,

248
00:08:23,753 --> 00:08:25,140
and you get the curl.

249
00:08:25,140 --> 00:08:27,210
It is a memory trick, not a meaning.

250
00:08:27,720 --> 00:08:29,140
The meaning is the drop of

251
00:08:29,180 --> 00:08:30,500
dye, and the paddle wheel.

252
00:08:32,480 --> 00:08:34,540
So far, everything has been tiny.

253
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:36,120
Now let's zoom out.

254
00:08:36,980 --> 00:08:38,660
Take a whole region, and

255
00:08:38,740 --> 00:08:40,130
cut it into small squares.

256
00:08:40,360 --> 00:08:42,953
Each square has its own outflow.

257
00:08:43,007 --> 00:08:44,750
Its divergence, times its area.

258
00:08:45,670 --> 00:08:48,093
Look at two neighboring squares.

259
00:08:48,227 --> 00:08:49,400
Whatever leaves the first through

260
00:08:49,480 --> 00:08:51,360
their shared edge enters the second.

261
00:08:51,840 --> 00:08:53,530
Out for one, in for the other.

262
00:08:53,620 --> 00:08:56,040
Add them, and that edge cancels.

263
00:08:56,800 --> 00:08:59,210
That happens on every inside edge.

264
00:08:59,520 --> 00:09:01,130
Every single one cancels.

265
00:09:01,390 --> 00:09:03,100
The only edges left are the

266
00:09:03,220 --> 00:09:04,350
ones on the outer boundary.

267
00:09:05,090 --> 00:09:06,720
So adding up the divergence

268
00:09:06,800 --> 00:09:08,880
inside a region gives you exactly

269
00:09:08,960 --> 00:09:10,600
the flow out through its boundary.

270
00:09:11,190 --> 00:09:12,880
In three dimensions, the squares

271
00:09:12,960 --> 00:09:14,480
become little cubes, and the

272
00:09:14,720 --> 00:09:16,960
boundary becomes a closed surface.

273
00:09:16,960 --> 00:09:18,540
This is the divergence theorem.

274
00:09:19,550 --> 00:09:21,690
And now the lamp from earlier pays off.

275
00:09:21,970 --> 00:09:24,340
In three dimensions, its arrows fade

276
00:09:24,420 --> 00:09:26,300
with the square of the distance.

277
00:09:26,340 --> 00:09:28,340
The same amount crosses every sphere

278
00:09:28,420 --> 00:09:30,260
around the center, so every bit of

279
00:09:30,380 --> 00:09:32,410
divergence sits at that one point.

280
00:09:32,630 --> 00:09:34,660
That is exactly how the electric

281
00:09:34,740 --> 00:09:36,330
field of a point charge behaves.

282
00:09:37,270 --> 00:09:38,740
Curl adds up the same way.

283
00:09:39,270 --> 00:09:41,000
Give each small square its own

284
00:09:41,160 --> 00:09:42,420
little loop of circulation.

285
00:09:42,960 --> 00:09:44,343
Where two squares meet, their

286
00:09:44,520 --> 00:09:45,880
loops run along the shared

287
00:09:45,960 --> 00:09:47,450
edge in opposite directions.

288
00:09:47,760 --> 00:09:48,600
They cancel again.

289
00:09:49,670 --> 00:09:51,300
Only the outer loop survives.

290
00:09:51,580 --> 00:09:53,620
The total curl inside equals

291
00:09:53,740 --> 00:09:55,350
the circulation around the edge.

292
00:09:55,680 --> 00:09:57,090
That is Green's theorem.

293
00:09:58,090 --> 00:10:00,000
Lift it into space, and the region

294
00:10:00,080 --> 00:10:01,920
can be any surface stretched across

295
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:04,400
a loop of wire, like a soap film.

296
00:10:04,400 --> 00:10:05,920
Push the film out into a bubble,

297
00:10:06,200 --> 00:10:07,720
and the answer does not change.

298
00:10:08,010 --> 00:10:09,330
Only the wire matters.

299
00:10:09,670 --> 00:10:11,100
This is Stokes' theorem.

300
00:10:12,020 --> 00:10:13,600
Two theorems, one idea.

301
00:10:13,830 --> 00:10:16,420
Add up the tiny answers inside, and

302
00:10:16,500 --> 00:10:18,430
everything cancels except the edge.

303
00:10:20,380 --> 00:10:21,760
Now, those four equations

304
00:10:21,840 --> 00:10:22,420
from the beginning.

305
00:10:22,870 --> 00:10:24,587
They are Maxwell's equations,

306
00:10:24,613 --> 00:10:25,880
and you can read them now.

307
00:10:26,590 --> 00:10:29,120
Del dot E equals rho over epsilon naught.

308
00:10:29,740 --> 00:10:31,380
Electric charges are the sources

309
00:10:31,460 --> 00:10:32,860
and sinks of the electric field.

310
00:10:33,980 --> 00:10:35,650
Del dot B equals zero.

311
00:10:35,860 --> 00:10:38,800
The magnetic field has no sources at all.

312
00:10:38,840 --> 00:10:40,650
Its lines never start or end.

313
00:10:40,910 --> 00:10:42,080
They only loop back.

314
00:10:42,880 --> 00:10:44,780
Del cross E equals minus

315
00:10:44,860 --> 00:10:46,240
the rate of change of B.

316
00:10:46,520 --> 00:10:48,300
A changing magnetic field

317
00:10:48,380 --> 00:10:50,050
stirs up an electric whirl.

318
00:10:50,320 --> 00:10:52,940
Spin a magnet near a coil of wire, and

319
00:10:53,020 --> 00:10:54,980
that whirl pushes a current around it.

320
00:10:55,330 --> 00:10:56,620
That is how most of our

321
00:10:56,700 --> 00:10:57,830
electricity is made.

322
00:10:59,030 --> 00:11:02,060
Del cross B equals mu naught J, plus

323
00:11:02,160 --> 00:11:04,240
a term for a changing electric field.

324
00:11:04,490 --> 00:11:06,160
Currents wrap themselves

325
00:11:06,240 --> 00:11:07,370
in magnetic whirls.

326
00:11:07,880 --> 00:11:10,160
And together, the last two equations let

327
00:11:10,240 --> 00:11:13,120
a changing E make B, and a changing B

328
00:11:13,280 --> 00:11:16,150
make E, so the pair can travel on its own.

329
00:11:16,630 --> 00:11:17,840
That is light.

330
00:11:18,880 --> 00:11:20,180
The same two questions show

331
00:11:20,260 --> 00:11:21,590
up far from electricity.

332
00:11:21,860 --> 00:11:24,100
Water barely compresses, so

333
00:11:24,180 --> 00:11:25,980
its flow has zero divergence.

334
00:11:26,120 --> 00:11:27,700
And a hurricane, seen from

335
00:11:27,780 --> 00:11:30,280
space, is a giant patch of curl.

336
00:11:32,370 --> 00:11:33,120
Let's go back to the

337
00:11:33,200 --> 00:11:34,150
stream we started with.

338
00:11:35,360 --> 00:11:36,420
Here is the divergence

339
00:11:36,500 --> 00:11:38,010
map, laid over the flow.

340
00:11:38,350 --> 00:11:39,940
The place where the dust spread

341
00:11:40,060 --> 00:11:42,140
apart sits right on a warm spot.

342
00:11:42,540 --> 00:11:44,020
And here is the curl.

343
00:11:44,280 --> 00:11:45,940
The place where the dust started

344
00:11:46,060 --> 00:11:47,780
turning is this green swirl.

345
00:11:48,840 --> 00:11:50,600
Divergence asks how much is

346
00:11:50,680 --> 00:11:52,710
leaving a point, per unit of area.

347
00:11:53,380 --> 00:11:55,670
Curl asks how much is turning around it.

348
00:11:56,100 --> 00:11:58,170
Add up either one over a region,

349
00:11:58,390 --> 00:12:00,160
and only the boundary is left.

350
00:12:01,180 --> 00:12:02,380
The formulas you memorized

351
00:12:02,460 --> 00:12:03,500
were never the point.

352
00:12:03,980 --> 00:12:05,420
They are the drop of dye and the

353
00:12:05,500 --> 00:12:07,460
paddle wheel, written in symbols.

354
00:12:08,440 --> 00:12:09,640
Next time, we will take the

355
00:12:09,680 --> 00:12:11,080
divergence of a gradient.

356
00:12:11,370 --> 00:12:13,480
It is called the Laplacian, and it

357
00:12:13,520 --> 00:12:15,920
is hiding inside heat, sound, and

358
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:18,320
almost every equation in physics.
